The JS reference disposes on a scope's closing brace and on `return`, but NOT
on `break`: a `disposable` declared in a loop body leaks on the iteration that
breaks out. The bootstrap disposes there too.

Reproducing a leak to match would not be parity worth having, so this fixture
asserts the bootstrap's behaviour alone. See dispose_stack.yoop.

That covers the SWITCH cases at the end too: a `break` out of a switch nested in
a loop unwinds the arm's scope and stops, and a `continue` in the same arm
unwinds out to the loop. The reference disposes on neither, so both halves are
bootstrap-only for the same one reason.
